## 1. Introduction
As a carbon-neutral energy carrier alternative to fossil fuels, molecular hydrogen attracts wide attention due to its environmentally friendliness and high energy efficiency [1]. Currently, steam reforming and coal gasification can produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) on an industrial scale, but these commercial processes will incur \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emission, leading to a warming climate [2,3]. A sustainable approach to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with high purity is the electrochemical water splitting powered by the intermittent energy, such as wind and sun [4,5]. This process involves in two "semi-reactions": hydrogen evolution and oxygen evolution [6,7]. At present, the state-of-the-art catalyst for HER is Pt, but its large-scale utilization in water splitting as HER catalysts was commercially impossible due to its exorbitant price and scarcity [8,9]. Therefore, to develop HER catalysts with low cost and high abundance is highly imperative for the commercialization of water splitting.
As a carbon-neutral energy carrier alternative to fossil fuels, molecular hydrogen attracts wide attention due to its environmentally friendliness and high energy efficiency [1]. Currently, steam reforming and coal gasification can produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) on an industrial scale, but these commercial processes will incur \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) emission, leading to a warming climate [2,3]. A sustainable approach to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) with high purity is the electrochemical water splitting powered by the intermittent energy, such as wind and sun [4,5]. This process involves in two "semi-reactions": hydrogen evolution and oxygen evolution [6,7]. At present, the state-of-the-art catalyst for HER is Pt, but its large-scale utilization in water splitting as HER catalysts was commercially impossible due to its exorbitant price and scarcity [8,9]. Therefore, to develop HER catalysts with low cost and high abundance is highly imperative for the commercialization of water splitting.Up to now, a myriad of earth-abundant transition metal catalysts have been pursued to improve the HER performance [10], including transition-metal carbides [11,12], sulfides [13], oxides [14], phosphides [15-17] and selenides [18]. Transition-metal phosphide (TMP) features a high conductivity as a metalloid alloyed with metal and phosphorus, and it is particularly well suited for use as a HER catalyst over a wide pH range due to its high resistance to acid/base [19]. This merit will expand its application as HER catalysts in energy storage and conversion. HER activity for the most of TMPs in alkaline electrolyte was significantly inferior to that in acidic solution [20,21]. The pH-dependent HER activity is related to the difference of the HER pathway between alkaline and acid solutions [22,23]. The discharge of H intermediate from water in alkaline electrolyte was slower than that from hydronium ions \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}\right)\)in acid solution, which can account for a sluggish HER kinetics in alkaline electrolyte compared with that in acid solution [23]. Among such TMPs, Co phosphides (CoP) attract more research interest as HER catalysts alternative to the commercial Pt catalysts [15,24,25]. Despite the effort made to optimize CoP structure to generate abundant accessible active sites, the HER overpotential at CoP catalysts is still relatively high in comparison with Pt catalysts, especially over a wide pH range [19,26]. Therefore, there still remains a challenge to develop efficient HER catalysts with robust stability at all pH values [27,28].Many works have demonstrated that the HER activity of CoP can be significantly enhanced in different pH range by introducing the electroactive Fe, Ni and Mn atom [27,29-31]. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations reveal that heteroatom doping can weaken the electron interactions between Co and H atoms, leading to thermo-neutral free energy \(\left(\Delta\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{H}}\right)\) for hydrogen adsorption [19,24,32,33]. Mo introduction has been proven to be effective to boost HER performance of TMPs. However, the promoting effect of Mo is not clear in the HER pathway at the TMP. Li L et al believed that Mo atoms play a role as doped heteroatom in CoP, which can efficiently modify the electronic structure of CoP for hydrogen adsorption [19]. Yu L et al suggested that the promoting effect of Mo for HER may derive from the synergistic effect between Ni and Mo atoms. The Ni atom can act as water dissociation sites and Mo atom is favorable for hydrogen adsorption [34]. On the other hand, HER occurs on the surface of catalysts. Therefore, the hollow micro/nanostructure will possess large surface area and multiple interfaces [35,36]. These structural advantages make it more available to HER. Moreover, the 3D architecture can reduce diffusion lengths for mass transport, leading to a fast HER kinetics at CoP. From these points of view, the CoP based catalysts should be designed with 3D hollow micro/nanostructure to increase the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) for HER over a wide pH range.
Many works have demonstrated that the HER activity of CoP can be significantly enhanced in different pH range by introducing the electroactive Fe, Ni and Mn atom [27,29-31]. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations reveal that heteroatom doping can weaken the electron interactions between Co and H atoms, leading to thermo-neutral free energy \(\left(\Delta\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{H}}\right)\) for hydrogen adsorption [19,24,32,33]. Mo introduction has been proven to be effective to boost HER performance of TMPs. However, the promoting effect of Mo is not clear in the HER pathway at the TMP. Li L et al believed that Mo atoms play a role as doped heteroatom in CoP, which can efficiently modify the electronic structure of CoP for hydrogen adsorption [19]. Yu L et al suggested that the promoting effect of Mo for HER may derive from the synergistic effect between Ni and Mo atoms. The Ni atom can act as water dissociation sites and Mo atom is favorable for hydrogen adsorption [34]. On the other hand, HER occurs on the surface of catalysts. Therefore, the hollow micro/nanostructure will possess large surface area and multiple interfaces [35,36]. These structural advantages make it more available to HER. Moreover, the 3D architecture can reduce diffusion lengths for mass transport, leading to a fast HER kinetics at CoP. From these points of view, the CoP based catalysts should be designed with 3D hollow micro/nanostructure to increase the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) for HER over a wide pH range.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOF) prove an ideal template for the preparation of 3D hollow micro/nanostructured materials due to its high surface area and well-defined configuration [37-39]. Herein, a series of CoP·xCoMoP NCs were prepared using zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF-67) as template by tuning Mo to Co ratio via a dissolution-regrowth, and subsequent phosphorization of the pre-synthesized \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Mo}_{y}\mathrm{O}_{z}\) nanocages \((\mathbf{x}+3\mathbf{y}=z\) , \(\mathrm{Co}_{\mathrm{x}}.\) - \(\mathrm{Mo}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs). At an optimum ratio of Mo to Co (5:6), CoP·xCoMoP NCs retain the inherent polyhedral morphology of ZIF-67, and possess a homologous heterojunction with CoP and CoMoP phase. The as-prepared CoP·xCoMoP NCs show superior HER activity and durability over a wide pH range that can be comparable to the state-of-the-art TMPs reported recently.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-67 nanocrystals
2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-67 nanocrystalsThe ZIF-67 nanocrystals were synthesized by following the reported procedures with modification [39,40]. In a typical procedure, 2-methylimidazole \((1.64\,\mathrm{~g})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((1.455\,\mathrm{~g})\) were dissolved in methanol \((100\,\mathrm{~mL})\), and mixed together under ultrasonic agitation. The mixture was stirred for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) and then allowed to stand at room temperature for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\). Afterwards, the ZIF-67 nanocrystals were separated by centrifugation, washed with ethanol for at least 3 times, and dried overnight at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2.2. Synthesis of \(\mathsf{C o}_{x}\mathsf{M o}_{y}\mathsf{O}_{z}\) NCs
\(\mathsf{C o}_{x}\mathsf{M o}_{y}\mathsf{O}_{z}\) NCs were prepared according to the reference [35]. In brief, ZIF-67 nanocrystals \((50\,\mathrm{~mg})\) were ultrasonically dispersed into ethanol \((20\,\mathrm{~mL})\), and a certain amount of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\) was dissolved in the deionized water \((80\,\mathrm{~mL})\). \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\) solution was slowly mixed with the dispersion system of ZIF-67. The formation of \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Mo}_{y}\mathrm{O}_{z}\) nanocages involves in a dissolution–regrowth process [41]. More to the point, \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) ions were initially released from ZIF-67 with assistance of water introduced by \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\) solution. Then, the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) ions will diffuse outward to form \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Mo}_{y}\mathrm{O}_{z}\) shells, and meanwhile, voids will be created inside with the dissolution of ZIF-67. Finally, the well-defined \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Mo}_{y}\mathrm{O}_{z}\) NCs will produce after the complete corrosion of ZIF-67. The mixture was refluxed at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(60\,\mathrm{~min}\), and the green powder was collected by centrifugation. Then, they were washed with ethanol and water repeatedly. The \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{Mo}_{y}\mathrm{O}_{z}\) NCs were dried over night at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in vacuum.
