## 2.5. Electrochemical measurements
In this work, the electrochemical performance was carried out on a CHI760C electrochemical workstation equipped with a typical three-electrode setup. A saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and graphite disk electrode were used as the reference electrode and the counter electrode, respectively. The glassy carbon electrode (GCE, diameter: \(3\ \mathrm{mm}\) ) acted as the working electrode. To modify GCE with the as-prepared samples, \(5.0~\mathrm{mg}\) of the as-prepared samples is dispersed in a mixed solution containing \(50\,\textrm{\textmu L}\) of ethanol, \(47~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of deionized water and \(3~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of nafion \((5\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\) , and ultrasonically treated for at least \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) to prepare a uniform catalyst ink. Then \(6~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of catalyst ink was dropped on the GCE with a pipette, and dried under an infrared lamp. The mass loading of the catalysts is calculated to be \(0.3\;\mathrm{mg}\) on the GCE. The electrolyte, such as \(1.0\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution, \(0.5\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution and \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) PBS, was saturated with \(\Nu_{2}\) before the electrochemical characterization.
Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was carried out with a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}\) . All of the LSV curves were recorded after IRcorrection. The potentials were referred to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) on the basis of the equation: \(\mathrm{E_{RHE}\,=\,E_{S C E}\,+\,0.059}\) \(\times\ \mathrm{pH}\,+\,0.242\ \mathrm{V}\) [33]. The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was calculated from the capacitive current in the nonfaradaic area against the scan rate [5]. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed at the overpotential of \(100~\mathrm{mV}\) in a frequency range from 0.01 to \(100~\mathrm{kHz}\) with an AC voltage of \(5~\mathrm{mV}\) . The long-term stability of the electrocatalysts was tested by the chronoamperometry (CA). The LSV curves were recorded before and after the CA tests to evaluate the stability of the electrocatalysts.
## 2.6. DFT calculation
Theory calculations were performed using Vienna ab-initio simulation package (VASP) [42]. The electron-ion interactions were modeled by projector augmented wave (PAW) potentials [43]. The exchange-correlation effect was analyzed using the PerdewBurke-Ernzerhof (PBE) scheme on the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) [44]. The cut-off energy based on a plane-wave was set at \(400\,\mathrm{eV}\) all calculations, and a vacuum layer of \(15\,\mathrm{\AA}\) is applied for all calculated models [32].
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 shows the morphology and composition of \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs imaged by SEM and TEM. \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs will retain the configuration of ZIF-67 as a rhombic dodecahedron with an average size of \(300{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1A). Different form a smooth solid of ZIF-67 (Figure S1), \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs show a hollow structure integrated with continuous ultrathin nanosheets which thickness is less than \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1B). The Co, Mo and O elements were uniformly distributed among the entire of \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs (Fig. 1C, 1D and 1E). The presence of C and N elements indicates the preservation of ZIF-67 frame even after the formation of \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs (Fig. 1F and 1G). The composition of \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs was further determined by ICP-AES, giving the atom ratio of Co and Mo to be 6:5 (Table S1). Therefore, the \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{5}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{5}}\mathsf{O}_{21}\) is referred to as
\(\mathsf{C o O.5C o M o O_{4}}\) . For such perspective, the \(\mathsf{C o O.5C o M o O_{4}}\) NCs form on ZIF-67 in the process of dissolution-regrowth [35]. TEM images also confirm a hollow structure of \(\mathsf{C o O.5C o M o O_{4}}\) NCs (Fig. 1H). The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image of \(\mathsf{C o O.5C o M o O_{4}}\) NCs shows that an interplanar space of the lattice fringe was determined to be 0.42 and \(0.45~\mathrm{nm}\) , which is corresponding to (111) and (002) plane of \(\mathsf{C o M o O_{4}}\) phase [23], respectively (Fig. 1I).
The formation of CoP.5CoMoP NCs involves in a heat-assisted phosphating process using CoO.5CoMoO4 NCs as precursors. CoP.5CoMoP NCs still preserve hollow structure wrapped by nanosheets with a thickness of less than \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 2A and 2B). The composition analysis on CoP.5CoMoP NCs shows the uniform distribution of Co, Mo and P elements (Fig. 2C, 2D and 2E). The existence of C and N elements suggests the formation of amorphous carbon in the carbonization process of ZIF-67 frame (Fig. 2F and 2H). In addition, the O element can be observed, which may be attributed to the oxygenated groups on amorphous carbon (Fig. 2G). TEM image reveals a hollow structure of CoP.5CoMoP NCs similar to \(\mathsf{C o O.5C o M o O_{4}}\) NCs (Fig. 2I). The HRTEM image from the nanosheets reveals the two-crystal system of metal phosphide (Fig. 2J). A lattice fringe with an interplanar space of 0.25 and \(0.28~\mathrm{~nm}\) is indexed to (111) and (011) plane of CoP phase [17,19], respectively. Besides, another lattice fringe with an interplanar space of \(0.23\ \mathrm{nm}\) is assigned to the (112) plane of CoMoP phase [44]. The morphological evolution of CoP.xCoMoP NCs and the precursor of \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs was investigated by SEM (Figure S2). The atom ratio of Co and Mo can be controlled by the amount of \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}\) in the process of dissolution-regrowth. All the CoP.xCoMoP NCs and \(\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{M o}_{\mathrm{y}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathrm{z}}\) NCs were shown as hollow polyhedron. In comparison, \(\mathsf{C o O.5C o M o O_{4}}\) NCs remain intact, while some of CoO.7CoMoO4 NCs and CoO.3CoMoO4 NCs are broken. Therefore, after phosphating, CoP.5CoMoP NCs were presented as rhombic dodecahedron, which is relative intact compared to CoP.7CoMoP NCs and CoP.3CoMoP NCs. These intact CoP.5CoMoP NCs will be more advantageous in the specific surface area than those cracked CoP.7CoMoP NCs and CoP.3CoMoP NCs. For comparison, the pure CoPs were also prepared by direct phosphating of ZIF-67 (Figure S3). The SEM image of the pure CoPs was shown as nanoparticles without a specific shape. TEM images further reveal a hollow structure for CoP nanoparticles (CoP HPs), and the HRTEM image confirms the presence of CoP phase.
