The graphitization degree of amorphous carbon in CoP·5CoMoP NCs and CoP HPs is studied by Raman spectra (Fig. 3C). Two peaks at about 1360 and \(1605\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in Raman spectra correspond to the D and G bands, respectively. The intensity ratio \(\left(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{D}}/\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}\right)\) between the D and G bands is 0.86 for CoP·5CoMoP NCs and 1.02 for CoP HPs. For the CoP·5CoMoP NCs, the lower \(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{D}}/\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}\) value suggests a higher graphitization of amorphous carbon, which will favor electron transfer in consequence due to a higher electric conductivity [47-49].
The chemical valence states of CoO·5CoMoO4 NCs, CoP·5CoMoP NCs and CoP HPs were characterized by XPS measurements. Fig. 3D shows the core level Mo 3d XPS of CoO·5CoMoO4 NCs and CoP·5CoMoP NCs. Only a couple of Mo (VI) 3d peaks can be observed from the XPS of CoO·5CoMoO4 NCs at \(231.9\ \mathrm{eV}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Mo}^{\mathrm{VI}}\right.\) 3d 5/2) and \(234.9\ \mathrm{eV}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Mo}^{\mathrm{VI}}\right.\) 3d 3/2). By contrast, there are three couples of peaks at the XPS of CoP·5CoMoP NCs. A dominant couple
of peaks were emerged at \(229.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) and \(232.6\ \mathrm{eV}\), which can be attributed from the \(\mathrm{Mo}^{\delta}\) species \((0<\delta\) less than 4) due to the formation of CoMoP phase [44]. On the other hand, the XPS intensity of Mo (VI) species was diminished, and a small couple of peaks from the Mo V species were presented at \(230.6\ \mathrm{Mo}^{\mathrm{V}}\ 3\mathrm{d}\ 5/2\) and \(233.6\ \mathrm{eV}\) \(\left(\mathrm{Mo}^{\mathrm{V}}\ 3\mathrm{d}\ 3/2\right)\). The Mo IV and Mo V species may be derived from the surface oxidation of CoMoP [45]. The high-resolution Co 2p XPS shown in Fig. 3E can be deconvoluted into two couples of peaks, a main couple is corresponding to \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) species, and another is derived from its satellite peaks. For \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) species, its Co 2p 3/2 XPS peak was located at \(780.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) and Co 2p 1/2 XPS peak at \(796.6\ \mathrm{eV}[50]\). After phosphorization, the Co 2p 3/2 XPS peak of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) species in CoP·5CoMoP NCs shifts positive up to \(781.6\ \mathrm{eV}\), in the meantime, a new couple was presented at \(778.7\ \mathrm{eV}\) and \(793.6\ \mathrm{eV}\), which can be attributed to Co 2p 3/2 and 2p 1/2 XPS spectrum of \(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{O}}\) species, respectively. The binding energy of \(\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{O}}\) species in CoP·5CoMoP NCs shifts positively by \(0.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) in comparison with that in CoP HPs. In addition, the curve of P 2p XPS can be fitted as three peaks (Fig. 3F): A couple at \(129.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) and \(130.1\ \mathrm{eV}\) is corresponding to the P 2p 3/2 and P 2p 1/2 XPS of metal phosphide, respectively[51]. A broad peaks at \(133.4\ \mathrm{eV}\) may generate from the surface oxidized P—O species [19]. The P 2p 3/2 XPS of M—P bond in \(\mathrm{Mo}_{0.83}\)-CoP NCs shows a negative shift of \(0.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) in contrast to that of CoP HPs. Therefore, Mo doping in CoP phase will induce the shift of the binding energy of Co and P elements, leading to a modification on the electronic structure of CoP lattice [52]. The
