lysts, which is smallest among the CoP.xCoMoP NC catalysts and almost one half of that for CoP HP catalysts. Obviously, Mo introduction favors HER kinetics at CoP.xCoMoP NC catalysts. To understand the fundamental mechanism of enhanced HER performance at CoP.xCoMoP NC catalysts, the ECSA value was evaluated by the electrochemical double-layer capacitance \(\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)\) , which was measured from the current difference between charge and discharge in the non-faradaic region of the CV curves with the different scan rates (Figure S4). As shown in Fig. 4C, CoP.5CoMoP NC catalysts possess a largest \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\) value among CoP.xCoMoP NCs, in good agreement with the SEM observation. The \(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\) value of CoP.5CoMoP NC catalysts is also far above that of CoP HP catalysts. The reason can be well explained by the larger specific surface area of CoP.5CoMoP NCs determined by the BET measurement, as well as the higher graphitization degree of amorphous carbon in CoP.5CoMoP NCs revealed by Raman spectra. To gain an insight into the HER kinetics at CoP.xCoMoP NC catalysts, the EIS measurements were carried out at the overpotential of \(100~\mathrm{\mV}.\) The Nyquist plots were fitted with an equivalent circuit in the inset of Fig. 4D. All the Nyquist plots were presented as a semicircle, which was associated with the charge resistance \(\left(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{CT}}\right)\) [23]. The CoP.5CoMoP NC catalysts show a smaller \(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{CT}}\) value of \(32\,\Omega\) among
the CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts, far lower than that \((220\ \Omega)\) of CoP HP catalysts, which suggests a fast HER kinetics at CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts. The integration of Mo into CoP lattice creates a heterostructured interface between CoP and CoMoP phase in CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts as revealed by TEM and XRD. The building Mo atoms in the CoP lattice show multiple valences. This alloying effect can tune the electronic structure of the neighboring Co and P atom, leading to lattice mismatch and distortion in the CoP crystal [17]. More importantly, these lattice defects will be available to HER as active sites, which account for a large ECSA value for CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts. On the other hand, the intercept of Nyquist plots at X-axis provides the series resistance \(\left(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{s}}\right)\) of HER catalysts [53]. The \(\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{s}}\) value of CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts is about \(6\ \Omega\), less than that \((14\ \Omega)\) of CoP HP catalysts, which indicates a higher intrinsic conductivity for CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts compared with CoP HP catalysts. The high conductivity of CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts favors electron transfer, which also contributed much to a high ECSA value for CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts.
The demand for the electrochemical water splitting is diversified toward pH value of electrolyte solution in practices. Some water electrolysis technologies will produce hydrogen with proton exchange membrane or in the presence of microbial [27]. Therefore, an efficient HER electrocatalysts will work in strong acidic or neutral solution. Fig. 5A shows the IR-corrected LSV curves of CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts with CoP HP and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts as controls in \(0.5\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution \(\left(\mathrm{pH}\ 0.29\right)\) at a scan rate of \(5\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\). The CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts can deliver a cathodic current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at a lowest overpotential of \(44\ \mathrm{mV}\) among the CoP·xCoMoP NC catalysts. The overpotential at the current density of \(10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) for CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts is only \(33\ \mathrm{mV}\) above the commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts, but almost one quarter of the CoP HP catalysts. Fig. 5B gives a corresponding Tafel slope of \(108.8\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) for the CoP HP catalysts, indicative of a Volmer reaction for HER mechanism at CoP HP catalysts. By comparison, the Tafel slope of CoP·5CoMoP NC at \(10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) was calculated to be \(51.2\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}\), suggesting a HER mechanism ruled by Volmer-Heyrovsky reaction at CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts [32].
Obviously, the difference of HER mechanism between CoP·5CoMoP NC and CoP HP catalysts was induced by Mo introduction. As discussed above, Mo introduction can modify the electronic structure of CoP lattice and lower the energy barrier for proton adsorption, leading to a different HER pathway at CoP lattice. On the other hand, the built-in Mo atoms in CoP lattice can also act as active sites for proton adsorption at the heterostructure between CoP and CoMoP lattice. Thus, the HER kinetics at CoP lattice was also changed as a result from the synergistic effect between CoP and CoMoP, resulting in a favorable mechanism to form \(\mathrm{H-H}\) bond for hydrogen evolution [34]. The remarkable HER performance of CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts was associated with the huge ECSA value in acidic electrolyte (Figure S5). The fast HER kinetics at CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts was further confirmed by the Nyquist plots, which gave a small \(\mathrm{R_{H}}\) value of \(5\,\Omega\) , far below that \((62\ \Omega)\) of the CoP HP catalysts (Figure S6). CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts also exhibit a high HER activity in the neutral solution. Fig. 5C shows that the overpotential at the current density of \(10\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) is only \(151~\mathrm{mV}\) for CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts in 1 M PBS, lower than those for other counterparts. The corresponding Tafel slope was calculated to be \(74.5~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}\) for CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts, as shown in Fig. 5D, second only to the commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalysts \((57.8\mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~mV~}~\,~dec^{-1}})\) . Similarly, the superior HER activity of CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts can be ascribed to their large ECSA value in the neutral media (Figure S7) and a favorable HER kinetics confirmed by the EIS measurements (Figure S8). The HER activity of CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts is compared with the state-of-the-art TMP catalysts listed in Table S2, which is no less than or even superior to most of noble metal-free HER electrocatalysts reported recently.
For the (1 1 1) face of CoMoP lattice, the \(\Delta\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{H^{+}}}\) value of the Co-site at the (1 1 1) face increases to \(-0.20~\mathrm{eV}\) , while the \(\Delta\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{H^{+}}}\) value of P sites at the (1 1 1) face drops dramatically to \(0.07~\mathrm{eV}\) . Obviously, Mo introduction will bring the \(\Delta\mathbf{G}_{\mathrm{H^{+}}}\) value at the (1 1 1) face of CoP lattice closer to zero, and thus greatly enhancing the HER activity at (1 1 1) face for CoP lattice, in good agreement with the experimental results. The HER in alkaline and neutral media will undergo a Volmer step for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) adsorption and activation in addition to the Heyrovsky process. The presence of Mo will reduce the water adsorption energy, favor the water activation via synergetic effect to cleave \(\mathrm{O-H}\) band and form H atom at the active sites [34]. Therefore, CoP·5CoMoP will show superior HER activity even in alkaline and neutral media.
The electrochemical stability of the HER catalyst over a wide pH range is another main concern for the commercialization of water electrolyzers [54]. The bulk electrolysis was performed at the overpotential of \(100\;\mathrm{mV}\) in the electrolyte solution with a different pH value, as shown in Figure 7A, a slight increase in the overpotential of HER can be observed form the CA curves of CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts after more than \(21\,\mathrm{{h}}\) test, demonstrating a robust durability for HER over a wide pH range. The magnified portion of the CA curves shows a zigzagged fluctuation as a result of accumulation and release of hydrogen gas on the surface of CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts [55]. After long-term electrolysis, the HER current can be stabilized at about \(40\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) in \(0.5\;\mathrm{M}\;\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, \(14\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) in 1 M KOH solution and \(2\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) in 1 M PBS, respectively, indicating a fairly high retention for HER activity at CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts. Fig. 7B shows the LSV curves recorded before and after the CA tests. No significant shift of HER overpotential can be observed at \(10\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\) . The corresponding Tafel slope is only increased by \(4\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in \(0.5\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, \(2\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in 1 M KOH solution, and \(1\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) in 1 M PBS (Figure S5), respectively, further confirming a considerably high HER stability at CoP·5CoMoP NC catalysts in a wide pH range. The high durability of CoP·5CoMoP NC may be attributed to amorphous carbon support, which inhibits the aggregation of TMP crystals, and thus ensures the structural stability of CoP·5CoMoP NCs during longterm electrolysis [52]. The morphology and composition of CoP·5CoMoP NCs after the durability test was imaged by SEM, which show no much significant changes (Figure S10). The atom ratio of Co and Mo in CoP·5CoMoP NCs still remains unchanged, which was determined to be 6:5 by ICP-AES after the durability
test. All the measurements confirm a robust durability toward HER at CoP-5CoMoP NC catalysts.
## 4. Conclusion
The synthesis of CoP·xCoMoP NCs involves in a dissolution-regrowth and subsequent phosphorization process using MOF as template. By tuning Mo to Co ratio, CoP-5CoMoP NC shows a superior HER activity and a fairly high long-term stability in all pH electrolytes. CoP·5CoMoP NCs only require 44, 72 and \(151~\mathrm{mV}\) overpotentials to reach a current density of \(10~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}\) in \(0.5~\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) solution, \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution and \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) PBS, respectively. CoP·5CoMoP NCs have eminent HER activity over a wide pH range due to the unique characteristics of its structure and composition: First of all, the 3D hollow micro/nanostructure possesses a large surface area and allows a fast charge transport. Secondly, Mo introduction not only provides more active sites for proton adsorption, but also reduces the energy barrier of proton adsorption. Finally, the synergistic effect between CoP and CoMoP can enhance HER catalytic activity. We believe that the simplicity and scalability of the synthesis for CoP·xCoMoP nanocages will provide inspiration for rational design of the pH-universal HER catalysts to expand their use in energy storage and conversion.
